{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner read the complete cloud successor-impact reconciliation 0d72ada6 and supporting assignment 82edb034. The checker owns its own report satisfaction; no planner ratification or duplicate four-point commission. The four actual successor details remain on existing R1/R3/R4 and I3/I5/IA owners: invitation-budget poisoning/shared overload behavior; member prose as data at the agent/tool boundary; human revocation and existing fork credential/hold policy; actual executable/image/configuration provenance and safe credential transport. Original cloud reports remain attributed old-4a code/run/inference evidence, not fresh source or live/provider certification. Planner has not personally read their complete bodies or executed their scripts.\n\nProduct disposition for the human revocation clarification is the already accepted R0 rows 27-30, not the historical review's immediate-cancel suggestion. Revoking one device while the controller has another active key leaves that member's hosted work available from the other device; the revoked device loses access within the chosen R3 windows. Removing the controller or revoking its last active key pauses hosted work, preserves it and stops new tools, fork-write dispatches and renewals within the applicable already chosen authority windows. An unexpired hold/token alone is not continuing authorization after that boundary. R3/I3/IA must name the exact applicable observation/clock/dispatch fences, existing issued-token revocation policy and later explicit-resume rules in the revised contract; no new numerical window or claim of immediate provider cancellation is adopted here. In-flight commands/pushes and unknown mint/revoke/deletion attempts keep their own identities and custody. Fencing future dispatch and a known token's expiry/revocation do not prove they ended. New device recovery is explicit; removal never restores the member.\n\nBounded direct reading of actual R3 private export/lifecycle/fork-seed c